ENT for node.exe spawn by npm. Hmm \"spawn d:\\Program Files\\nodejs\\node.exe ENOENT\" - it can't spawn node even though file exists. This may be a sandbox restriction: spawn with stdio pipe fails with EPERM in confined modes... but error is ENOENT. Actually in some sandboxes ENOENT is reported when execution is blocked. The earlier node --version worked, which spawns nothing further. npm needs to spawn node as child.\n\nOptions:\n1. Try running npm via cmd.exe /c npm install.\n2. Or install pptxgenjs via npm in non-captured mode? The pwsh tool itself captures stdout; the EPERM note is about child process spawning another program and capturing through piped stdio under confined mode. npm spawns node children - under a file sandbox pwsh runs in ConstrainedLanguage but node spawning... hmm but they note \"read-only pwsh runs in PowerShell ConstrainedLanguage mode, while workspace-write stays in FullLanguage\". We have workspace-write. In both confined modes, programs cannot open named pipes, so capturing another program's output via piped stdio fails with EPERM... and says \"that EPERM is the documented boundary: do not retry another way; escalate the exact command once or restructure.\"\n\nENOENT for spawn node.exe though... Might be because npm.cmd uses a shim to find node via \"%~dp0\\node.exe\"? d:\\Program Files\\nodejs\\npm.cmd would reference d:\\Program Files\\nodejs\\node.exe. That file exists. Spawn ENOENT could come from CreateProcess failing due to working directory being different drive? No.\n\nActually the workdir D:\\windoc\\desktop\\3\\ppt_build — the folder exists? Yes New-Item created it. Hmm.\n\nWait — maybe the pwsh tool ran in a session where a prior attempt to install node happened... Let's just try again, with cmd /c and see error. Alternatively use `& npm.cmd install pptxgenjs`. Or run via full path to npm-cli.js: `node \"d:\\Program Files\\nodejs\\node_modules\\npm\\bin\\npm-cli.js\" install pptxgenjs`.\n\nLet me check what node modules npm ships: d:\\Program Files\\nodejs\\node_modules\\npm\\bin\\npm-cli.js probably exists. Running `node npm-cli.js install` uses node directly as the process (no spawn of node for itself), though npm still spawns child processes for scripts — install of pptxgenjs typically no build scripts, might be OK.\n\nBut wait, could the ENOENT be that node is being spawned with stdio pipes and sandbox denies creating them → reports ENOENT? The documentation says EPERM though. But the message we got is from npm itself (npm reported \"Error: spawn ... ENOENT\"). npm's own spawn of node for... Hmm. Actually the first line of npm output \"Error: spawn d:\\Program Files\\nodejs\\node.exe ENOENT\" might come from npm trying to re-exec itself (npm re-executes via node for version >= 7: it spawns node process from npm-cli.js).\n\nGiven sandbox constraints with piped stdio between processes: pwsh capturing npm output (pipe to pwsh) fine; npm spawning node with piped stdio → denied → ENOENT-ish? The docs say EPERM explicitly for named pipe/capture. But ENOENT here might be Windows CreateProcess failure with a different code mapped.\n\nThe documented guidance: when a command is denied and wider mode would let it succeed, escalate immediately same turn with sandbox_permissions. But is this a \"denial\"? The error did not come with a [sandbox: ...] marker, so it's not a policy denial per se. It might genuinely be environment: D:\\Program Files\\nodejs — npm shim path. Possibly node is a symlink/junction or the directory path has an issue with 8.3 names.\n\nAlternative: run `node --version` inside workdir, `node \"d:\\Program Files\\nodejs\\node_modules\\npm\\bin\\npm-cli.js\" install pptxgenjs --no-audit --no-fund` with workdir ppt_build. This invokes node directly; npm then runs as in-process JS (npm v11 might still spawn node for lifecycle scripts). Let's attempt."}}},{"type":"chunk","time":1788943596504,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":1,"block":{"type":"tool-call","id":"call_00_9Zx5E62mS