{"type":"assistant/message","seq":126,"time":1788943641909,"data":{"turn":3,"step":12,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"reasoning","text":"The workspace D:\\windoc\\desktop\\3 contains: ppt_build (just created), 3d-map.html, cleanup_C_admin.cmd, export_park_overview.py, 园区总览报告_化工园区1.docx.\n\nNow install pptxgenjs in ppt_build with danger-full-access (child spawning needs it). Workdir exists now. Use absolute output path. Avoid `cd` reliance — pass workdir ppt_build. But the spawn powershell ENOENT under danger-full-access suggests npm tried to execute something through powershell which it cannot find? Actually error \"spawn C:\\WINDOWS\\System32\\WindowsPowerShell\\v1.0\\powershell.exe ENOENT\" though the file exists (Test-Path True!). Bizarre ENOENT for an existing file is typical of some process-creation restriction (job object, or when spawning from a process in a restricted token). Hmm. But node spawn EPERM earlier in sandbox; now with full access, spawning node probably succeeded, and the failure moved to spawning powershell — perhaps npm ran a script via cmd/powershell with a restricted environment? ENOENT for powershell.exe though exists... This looks like the classic issue where spawn of a GUI subsystem app or with a certain flag fails. Confusing.\n\nAlternative pragmatic approach: avoid npm entirely. We don't strictly need npm. Options:\n\n1. Download pptxgenjs package tarball and extract — but that's network + still node_modules needed as a plain folder; we could fetch the tarball via Invoke-WebRequest or web_fetch? pwsh web requests should work (network may be allowed). The package tarball from registry: https://registry.npmjs.org/pptxgenjs/-/pptxgenjs-3.12.0.tgz. Then extract with tar (Windows has bsdtar). This avoids child spawn. pptxgenjs has dependencies (jszip, @types/node, https, etc.). Actually pptxgenjs 3.12 dependencies: jszip, and optional. Let me recall: pptxgenjs package.json dependencies: \"https\": \"^1.0.0\", \"jszip\": \"^3.10.1\", \"typescript\"... Hmm it does have a runtime dependency on jszip at least. Downloading recursively could get messy but manageable: pptxgenjs deps: @types/node? no that's dev. Runtime deps per package: jszip. Also maybe \"express\"? No.\n\nLet me instead verify whether running node -e requiring from a locally placed pptxgenjs works. Plan: fetch pptxgenjs tarball + jszip tarball, extract both into node_modules, plus their transitive deps. jszip deps: lie? jszip 3.x dependencies: \"lie\": \"~3.3.0\", \"pako\": \"~1.0.2\", \"readable-stream\": \"~2.3.6\", \"setimmediate\": \"^1.0.5\". Ugh transitive tree.\n\nBetter: use npm but figure out why spawn fails. The failure is inherent to sandbox? Let's test spawn of cmd.exe under full access. Also test execFileSync node under full access (need escalate). Under danger-full-access, node spawn got past (error changed to powershell ENOENT), so node spawn works there. Now npm tried to spawn powershell.exe? That happens when npm runs scripts with script-shell default = cmd? On Windows npm uses cmd.exe via ComSpec. If ComSpec env missing, npm may fallback to powershell? Actually the npm error \"spawn C:\\WINDOWS\\...\\powershell.exe ENOENT\" — while pwsh's Test-Path shows the file exists. Could the full-access pwsh run under a different architecture/redirection where System32 is actually SysWOW64? If node is 32-bit... node at d:\\Program Files\\nodejs — probably 64-bit. If spawned from a 32-bit process, C:\\WINDOWS\\System32 redirects to SysWOW64, and powershell.exe exists there too normally...\n\nAlternatively set npm script-shell to cmd.exe explicitly, or just avoid scripts: pptxgenjs package probably has no install scripts → npm shouldn't spawn anything for install besides... Wait, why did npm spawn powershell at all? For running lifecycle scripts of dependencies during install — none should exist. Unless npm decided to run \"rebuild\"... Or pptxgenjs has postinstall? Hmm. Some npm versions spawn node to run arborist? No.\n\nMaybe simpler: npm config to disable scripts: --ignore-scripts. That prevents npm from executing any lifecycle scripts (including postinstall). The earlier spawn of powershell might have been for a preinstall/postinstall of pptxgenjs (check package.json). pptxgenjs package.json historically has no install scripts, but let me add --ignore-scripts anyway.\n\nLet me retry npm install under danger-full-access with --ignore-scripts."},{"type":"tool-call","id":"call_00_W44UWzd2avVC2TWjJhoQ5604","name":"pwsh","arguments":"{\"command\": \"node \\\"d:\\\\Program Files\\\\nodejs\\\\node_modules\\\\npm\\\\bin\\\\npm-cli.js\\\" install pptxgenjs --no-audit --no-fund --ignore-scripts --loglevel=error 2>&1 | Select-Object -Last 8; Write-Output \\\"EXIT=$LASTEXITCODE\\\"; Test-Path \\\"D:\\\\windoc\\\\desktop\\\\3\\\\ppt_build\\\\node_modules\\\\pptxgenjs\\\\package.json\\\"\", \"description\": \"Install pptxgenjs with scripts disabled\", \"timeoutMs\": 240000, \"workdir\": \"D:\\\\windoc\\\\desktop\\\\3\\\\ppt_build\", \"sandbox_permissions\": \"danger-full-access\", \"justification\": \"npm 安装需要 node 创建子进程以完成依赖树安装，文件沙箱此前以 EPERM 拒绝，需放开进程执行权限。\"}"}],"source":{"kind":"model","provider":"deepseek-official","model":"deepseek-v4-flash"},"id":"c77116d7-ee59-4d59-b6cc-41ce9bffcc5b"},"usage":{"inputTokens":62,"outputTokens":1340,"totalTokens":65914,"cacheReadTokens":64512,"reasoningTokens":1059},"stream":[{"type":"chunk","time":1788943629946,"chunk":{"type":"block-start","index":0,"blockType":"reasoning"}},{"type":"reasoning-chunks","time0":1788943629946,"index":0,"dt":[120,27,50,1,0,0,0,0,20,0,0,0,32,4,0,0,0,7,0,0,14,0,0,0,0,0,28,0,0,0,0,0,14,1,0,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,0,0,42,0,0,0,0,0,1,19,21,0,0,0,0,0,148,0,0,0,0,0,0,0,0,40,7,0,2,1,6,24,1,0,35,3,0,0,31,0,33,0,66,0,46,0,0,15,1,0,0,0,55,19,9,0,19,0,0,19,1,0,0,7,0,7,0,15,0,0,0,0,16,33,19,51,0,23,61,0,121,0,0,0,74,0,0,0,0,0,47,0,0,0,0,0,72,0,0,0,0,0,61,0,0,0,8,58,0,0,0,50,0,0,0,0,61,44,0,54,1,0,0,18,0,0,0,9,0,43,0,0,1,0,0,59,1,0,0,0,0,0,320,1